# A. Overview

This spec describes the lifecycle of a Transaction created after a Bid and an Ask are matched (Direct Match or Composite Match), through to ownership transfer.

## A.1. Payment ↔ Payout Structure (Direct vs Indirect)

A single Payment (buyer side) can be matched against N Payouts (seller side) — this is the Composite Match case from the Order Matching spec. The relationship branches based on total payment value:

| Case | Threshold | Seller agreements | Buyer agreements |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| Direct | Total value \> £30,000 | N payouts → N seller agreements (buyer signs directly with each seller; may involve more than 1 seller) | N agreements — buyer signs one per matched payout |
| Indirect | Total value ≤ £30,000 | N payouts → N seller agreements, but each seller signs with Cask Exchange (not directly with buyer) | 1 agreement — buyer signs once, with Cask Exchange |

Aggregation rule: When 1 payment matches N payouts, the deposit step (Step 2\) only begins once all N payouts have completed Step 1 (i.e. every seller agreement is admin-signed).

Cascade cancellation: If any one of the N seller agreements expires or is rejected (per Step 1's cancellation branches) → that payout is cancelled → the whole payment is cancelled → all other related payouts in the same match group are cancelled too, even if their agreements were already signed.

## A.2. Session & Transaction Field Model — Direct Multi-Transaction Case

### Entity structure

* CheckoutSession (checkoutSessionId) — top-level container. Holds status, currentStep, and session-level mirror fields (see "Non-aggregated fields" below).

* Transaction (child of session) — one per matched buy/sell pair: sellerId, askId, bidId, transactionPrice. Each transaction tracks its own signing progress independently via its own field set — no shared state between transactions in the same session.

  ### Lifecycle, in order

| \# | Event | Transaction-level fields | Session-level fields |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| 1 | Session created | sellerAgreementStatus \= awaiting\_signature, sellerAgreementDocuSignEnvelopeId set, sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt set | status \= pending, currentStep \= deposit\_payment |
| 2 | Seller signs | sellerAgreementStatus: awaiting\_signature → seller\_signed, sellerAgreementSignedAt set | — |
| 2b | Admin approves seller agreement | sellerAgreementStatus → signed, sellerAgreementAdminSignedAt \+ sellerAgreementAdminSignerId set | — |
| 3 | Admin requests seller update | sellerAgreementStatus → update\_requested, sellerAgreementSignedAt → null, sellerAgreementDocuSignEnvelopeId changes to a new envelope. sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt does NOT change. No fields exist for who/why/when the update was requested (seller side only) | — |
| 3b | Seller re-signs → repeats step 2 → 2b |  |  |
| 4 | All transactions reach sellerAgreementStatus \= signed → session ready for deposit; buyer pays deposit | — | status → deposit\_paid, currentStep → agreement\_signing |
| 4b | Deposit paid — buyer agreement fields initialize per transaction | buyerAgreementStatus \= sent, buyerAgreementSignatureDueAt set (first time) | — |
| 5 | Buyer signs | buyerAgreementStatus: sent → buyer\_signed, buyerAgreementSignedAt set, sellerAgreementReleaseFormUrl generated for the first time (note: generated on buyer sign, not seller admin-sign, despite the field name) | — |
| 6 | Admin requests buyer update | buyerAgreementStatus: buyer\_signed → sent (reset), buyerAgreementSignedAt → null, buyerAgreementDocuSignEnvelopeId changes, sellerAgreementReleaseFormUrl → null, buyerAgreementUpdateRequestedAt / Reason / ById set, buyerAgreementSignatureDueAt reset to request time \+ 3 days | buyerDocuSignStatus → update\_requested, same buyerAgreementUpdateRequestedAt / Reason / ById fields also set at session level |
| 6b | Buyer re-signs | buyerAgreementStatus → buyer\_signed, buyerAgreementSignedAt set again | — |
| 7 | Admin approves buyer agreement | buyerAgreementStatus → completed, buyerAgreementAdminSignedAt \+ buyerAgreementAdminSignerId set | — |
| 8 | All transactions reach buyerAgreementStatus \= completed | — | status → agreement\_signed, currentStep → invoice\_payment |

  ### Seller-side vs Buyer-side "update requested" — key differences

|  | Seller side | Buyer side |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| Signature timestamp | Cleared to null | Cleared to null |
| DocuSign envelope | New envelope issued | New envelope issued |
| Signing deadline | Unchanged | Reset to request time \+ 3 days |
| Who/why/when tracked? | No — no such fields exist | Yes — UpdateRequestedAt/Reason/ById, at both transaction and session level |
| Release form | n/a | sellerAgreementReleaseFormUrl cleared to null |
| Session-level status mirror | — | buyerDocuSignStatus → update\_requested |

  ### Session progression: gated by the slowest transaction

* Forward: session status/currentStep only advances once every transaction in the session reaches the required field state.

* Regression: if any single transaction gets pulled back into update\_requested after the session already advanced past that gate, the session status regresses too — even if the other transactions in the session haven't changed. Example: both transactions reach buyerAgreementStatus \= buyer\_signed → session \= agreement\_buyer\_signed. One transaction then gets an admin update request → session drops back to deposit\_paid, even though the other transaction's fields are untouched.

* If the update request happens before the session ever reached that gate, there's no regression — there's nothing to fall back from.

⚠️ Non-aggregated session fields — flag for QA

Two session-level fields do not aggregate (max/min) across transactions — they simply mirror whichever transaction was most recently updated:

* expiryDate (session)

* buyerDocuSignStatus (session)

This means the session-level value can look inconsistent with the state of other transactions in the same session — e.g. if transaction A's deadline is closer than transaction B's, but B was touched more recently, the session shows B's values, not the soonest/most-conservative one.

Open Question: Is this mirror-latest-touch behavior intentional, or a known gap?

Reject paths still apply per-transaction in the multi-transaction session model, with the same cascade behavior confirmed in A.1: rejecting 1 transaction's agreement cancels that payout → cancels the payment → cancels all related payouts in the same session.

## A.3. Indirect Multi-Transaction Case — Buyer Agreement Tracking

Applies to: Composite Match, Indirect case (total value ≤ £30,000, per Section A.1) — N transactions in 1 session, but only 1 buyer agreement (buyer signs once with Cask Exchange, not once per seller).

| Field | Behavior in Indirect multi-transaction case |
| :---- | :---- |
| buyerAgreementSignatureDueAt (transaction level) | null for every transaction. The per-transaction field from A.2 only applies to the Direct case (where buyer signs once per transaction). |
| Buyer signing progress | Tracked at session (top-level) fields instead: buyerDocuSignEnvelopeId, buyerDocuSignStatus, buyerDocuSignSignedAt, buyerDocuSignAdminSignedAt, buyerDocuSignAdminSignerId |

The transaction-level fields still exist in the schema (not removed) — they're simply left null for every transaction, same as buyerAgreementSignatureDueAt. All real buyer-side write activity happens at the session level only.

In A.2, session buyerDocuSignStatus was described as mirroring whichever transaction was most recently touched (non-aggregated, since Direct has N real buyer signatures). In the Indirect case, buyerDocuSignStatus has only one write source (the single session-level buyer signature), so no mirror/aggregation ambiguity applies.

# B. Transaction States

| FE Status | Meaning | Entered when |  |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| Deposit Payment | Match created, waiting for seller to sign | Match executed |  |
| Deposit Payment | Seller signed, waiting for buyer deposit | Seller signature confirmed |  |
| Agreement Signing | Deposit paid, waiting for buyer signature | Deposit confirmed |  |
| Invoice Payment | Buyer signed, waiting for remaining balance | Buyer signature confirmed |  |
| Ownership Transfer | Fully paid, waiting for admin action | Final payment confirmed |  |
| Completed | Ownership transferred | Admin confirms transfer |  |
| Failed | Transaction terminated before completion | See Section D |  |

# C. Step-by-Step Business Rules

## Step 1 — Seller Signs Agreement

![][image2]

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Trigger | Match executed (Direct or Composite) |
| Actor | Seller, then Admin |
| Signing deadline | sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt \= createdAt \+ 3 days |
| Seller rejects | sellerAgreementRejectedAt set → status \= cancelled |
| Seller signs | sellerAgreementSignedAt set (overwritten on re-sign) → status \= Pending Admin Review.  |
| Deadline passes with no seller action | Status \= expired — same outcome as Payout Cancelled (match voided, order returns to waitlist) |
| Seller cannot reject once already signed | Confirmed — no reject option once in Pending Admin Review |
| Admin requests update | Status \= update\_requested, loops back to Pending Seller Signature.  For single-transaction cases, sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt resets to now \+ 3 days. In the multi-transaction case (session with N transactions — see A.2), sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt does NOT reset on update-request. sellerAgreementSignedAt is cleared to null, and the DocuSign envelope ID changes. No field track who requested the update or why on the seller side. |
| If deadline passes again during update\_requested | Same Payout Expired outcome (retry deadline is treated the same as the original) |
| Admin rejects | sellerAgreementAdminRejectedAt set → that payout cancelled → cascades: payment cancelled → all related payouts in the same match group cancelled too (confirmed, same pattern as expiry — see Section D) |
| Admin signs (no reject, no update requested) | sellerAgreementAdminSignedAt set, plus sellerAgreementAdminSignerId → status \= Agreement Complete → proceeds to Step 2 (Buyer Deposit) |
| Status after | Deposit Payment |

## Step 2 — Buyer Pays Deposit

![][image3]

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Trigger | Seller agreement fully complete (sellerAgreementStatus \= "signed" and sellerAgreementAdminSignedAt set — i.e. Step 1's Agreement Complete) |
| Current behavior | No separate pending\_seller step exists. currentStep \= deposit\_payment from the moment the session is created (see A.2, Step 1). The deposit gate (all transactions must reach sellerAgreementStatus \= signed) is enforced as a business rule, not a distinct currentStep value. See Section H for Sophia's proposed pending\_seller step. |
| Payment method | Stripe |
| Payment succeeds | depositPaidAt set → advance to next step (Step 3: Buyer Signs Agreement) |
| Payment fails / not made | If current\_step is still deposit\_payment and expiryDate \< now → transaction cancelled (full void — match released, order back to waitlist, same outcome as Step 1's Payout Cancelled/Expired) |
| Deposit deadline | expiryDate \= transaction created \+ 3 days (confirmed — same reference point for both single-transaction and multi-transaction sessions) |
| Status after | Agreement Signing |

## Step 3 — Buyer Signs Agreement

![][image4]

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Trigger | Deposit paid (status \= "deposit\_paid") |
| currentStep | agreement\_signing — spans both buyer-sign and admin-review sub-states (same pattern as pending\_seller in Step 2\) |
| Signing deadline | expiryDate \= depositPaidAt \+ 3 days |
| Buyer rejects | buyerDocuSignRejectedAt set → transaction cancelled, status updated |
| Buyer signs | buyerDocuSignSignedAt set (overwritten on re-sign) → pending admin review |
| Deadline passes with no buyer action | Transaction cancelled |
| Admin requests update | buyerAgreementUpdateRequestedAt set → loops back to Pending buyer signature, expiryDate reset to now \+ 3 days |
| Admin rejects | buyerDocuSignAdminRejectedAt set → transaction cancelled, status updated |
| Admin signs | buyerDocuSignAdminSignedAt set → Agreement Complete → proceeds to Step 4 (Final Payment) |
| Status after | Invoice Payment |

## Step 4 — Buyer Pays Remaining Balance

![][image5]

### Case 1: Stripe Payment

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Status transition | status: agreement\_signed → invoice\_paid |
| Step transition | currentStep: invoice\_payment → ownership\_transfer |
| manualPayment\* fields | Not used — all remain null on both sides |
| Stripe confirmation timestamp | None found — no dedicated field recording when Stripe confirmed the payment |
| Payout status after payment | Still not\_ready |

### Case 2: Manual Payment

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Status transition | status: invoice\_submitted → invoice\_paid |
| Step transition | currentStep: invoice\_payment → ownership\_transfer |
| manualPaymentStatus | pending\_review → approved |
| manualPaymentReviewedAt | null → timestamp set (e.g. 2026-08-28T05:00:11.756Z) |
| manualPaymentReviewerId | null → admin ID set |
| manualPaymentEvidenceUrl, manualPaymentNotes | Already populated from the earlier proof-upload step; unchanged on approval |

⚠️ Flagged issue (needs bug-vs-feature check): manualPaymentStatus can currently be set to rejected, but status and currentStep do not change when this happens — the transaction appears stuck with no visible state transition. Needs confirmation from BE on whether this is intended (e.g. buyer expected to resubmit evidence silently) or a genuine gap.

Open Question: No Stripe-confirmation timestamp field found in Case 1 — is payment time meant to be pulled from Stripe's own records/webhook log rather than stored on this object? 

### expiryDate for this step

Same mechanism for both cases:

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Deadline calculation | expiryDate \= buyerDocuSignAdminSignedAt (top-level) \+ 3 days — same formula for Stripe and Manual, no path-specific logic |
| When it's set | Once, at the moment buyerDocuSignAdminSignedAt is set (Step 3, admin signs buyer agreement) — not re-set at any point during Step 4 |
| Behavior through the step | Stays unchanged across invoice\_payment → ownership\_transfer — no reset, no recalculation |
| Status after (both cases) | Ownership Transfer |

### Cancellation — expiry during invoice\_payment:

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Trigger | currentStep \= "invoice\_payment" and expiryDate \< now (deadline passed, no final payment) |
| Effect | Payment cancelled |
| Cascade | All payouts linked to that payment are also cancelled |
| Mechanics | Same cascade engine as Steps 1–3 (Payment ↔ Payout cascade) — see Expiration & Cascade Cancellation Handling doc, Section 3.2 (Payment expiry/cancellation cascades to all linked Payouts) |

## Step 5 — Admin Transfers Ownership

![][image6]

| Item | Detail |
| :---- | :---- |
| Trigger | Full payment confirmed |
| Actor | Admin |
| Action | Admin uploads the ownership transfer document and marks the step complete |
| Field set on upload | ownershipTransferDocumentUrl |
| Status transition | status → completed |
| currentStep | Unchanged — no further currentStep value after ownership\_transfer |
| System side-effect | On status \= completed, system auto-triggers payout: payoutStatus → processing, payoutReference and payoutMetadata generated from Stripe |
| Status after | Completed |

# D. Cancellation Points Summary

All cancellation outcomes below are full transaction cancellation — match released, order returns to waitlist. Confirmed as consistent across Steps 1–3.

| Step | Cancel trigger | Field set |
| :---- | :---- | :---- |
| 1 — Seller Agreement | Seller rejects | sellerAgreementRejectedAt |
| 1 — Seller Agreement | Deadline passed, no seller action | — |
| 1 — Seller Agreement | Admin rejects | sellerAgreementAdminRejectedAt |
| 2 — Deposit | Deadline passed, no payment | — |
| 3 — Buyer Agreement | Buyer rejects | buyerDocuSignRejectedAt |
| 3 — Buyer Agreement | Deadline passed, no buyer action | — |
| 3 — Buyer Agreement | Admin rejects | buyerDocuSignAdminRejectedAt |
| 4 — Final/Invoice Payment | Deadline passed (currentStep \= invoice\_payment, expiryDate \< now) | — |

# E. Suggestions — Proposed Improvements

| \# | Suggestion | Problem it solves | Current behavior (for contrast) |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |
| 1 | Reset sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt to null when seller signs (before admin review) | Prevents a signed agreement from expiring while sitting in admin review, or expiring right after signing | Currently not reset — sellerAgreementSignatureDueAt stays at its original value through admin review.  Concern: Is admin review fast enough that this rarely matters? |
| 2 | Add a distinct pending\_seller currentStep, with no expiryDate, active while Step 1 (seller sign \+ admin review) is in progress | Makes the "waiting on seller side" period explicit and inspectable via currentStep, rather than inferred from sellerAgreementStatus across N transactions | Currently currentStep \= deposit\_payment from session creation — the seller-side waiting period isn't reflected in currentStep at all |

